#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Find agent and build processes that run outside agents.slice and move them in.

Why this exists: the launchers (agent-cli, agent-confine) are user-writable
scripts. On 2026-09-08 an agent put a four-line "agent-confine" first on PATH
for its workers and two lanes ran in app.slice for hours while the launcher
audit passed. Placement (every tmux pane starts through agent-confine) confines
lanes before a launcher runs; this warden catches what still lands outside: a
shadowed launcher, a `systemd-run --user --scope` without --slice, a CLI
started by a desktop app, or a build a lane detached.

Modes
  --report    (default) list what would move; log it; change nothing
  --correct   move it
  --status    read state without locking or writing it, then list every agent
              and build process with its cgroup, env markers, scan age, skips
              and unmatched suspects
  --selftest  classification and planning against injected process records

Rules, per process, never per pane
  * Launched work carries AGENT_CONFINE=1 in its environment (agent-confine
    stamps it; children inherit). A marked process outside the slice is an
    escape whatever its name, and its marked descendants go with it.
  * Agent CLI names and mise install directories come from agent-tools.json.
    They classify what no launcher touched: a CLI a desktop app started.
  * A comm equal to a configured name is a candidate, never the decision
    (D010): it is an agent once its executable lies under that tool's own
    mise install directory, equals one of its configured executable paths,
    is a bundled CLI engine under a desktop prefix, or the tool names no
    install location at all. Each check is a path-prefix or exact match
    against the process's own resolved executable, never a bare substring or
    suffix, so a self-chosen writable path that merely contains an install
    fragment (a scratch directory holding a fake `node_modules/...`) never
    confirms. A package-path fragment, the dashboard's own weaker signal for
    display, never confirms a match the warden acts on automatically. An
    unreadable executable keeps the name. A node/bun/python-hosted CLI
    instead matches by a mise install path in argv[0] or argv[1]. Claude's
    embedded search mode (argv[0] "ugrep") is a helper, not a session. A
    bundled CLI under /opt (codex-desktop ships one) is an agent by name;
    the app around it is not.
  * Build tools match by comm or by the script they run. They are adopted only
    when the nearest marker above them is an agent or the tmux server; a
    desktop app, an excluded helper, or nothing leaves them alone (reported).
  * Descendants ride along with a root only from a bounded set: build tools,
    shells and interpreters, agent helper binaries, and executables under the
    toolchain directories (~/.cargo, ~/.rustup, mise, bun, pnpm, node_modules,
    rustlib, gcc, llvm). Anything else under an escaped lane stays where it
    is, and so does its subtree.
  * A process already inside agents.slice is never moved as a descendant.
  * Sessions sharing a scope: in a cgroup that holds several agent sessions,
    the earliest-started one is the owner and every other one is moved to its
    own scope, so every session holds one equal CPU share (owner's rule,
    2026-09-08). Ancestry is not required: headless workers (codex exec,
    `claude -p &`) are often reparented. Never split when a cgroup between the
    session and agents.slice carries a limit tighter than the slice's own; the
    check is repeated right before the move. AGENT_WARDEN_SPLIT_SESSIONS=0
    turns the split off. A nested launch through the shim already gets its
    own scope from agent-confine, so most sessions never need this.
  * Exclusions are matched on argv tokens before any `--` (a flag is a flag,
    a prompt is not). Excluded processes and their subtrees stay put.
  * Identity is a pidfd. Each process is re-read (identity, cgroup,
    classification) and a pidfd opened just before the move; the move passes
    the pidfds to systemd (StartTransientUnit PIDFDs= through libsystemd), so
    a pid that changes hands cannot be attached. If the root fails its recheck
    the whole tree is dropped and replanned next tick. Placement is verified
    after the move by cgroup and start time.

Memory: moving a process moves its future charges only. Pages it already owns
stay charged to the old cgroup (cgroup v2). The headroom guard skips moves when
agents.slice is above 90% of memory.max, or when the counters cannot be read,
and alerts; it promises nothing about OOM safety.

Runs from background.slice with its own small MemoryMax and a runtime cap so
it does not stall with the workload it corrects. State under
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/agent-warden/ sits behind a lock for writers. The status mode
reads state without that lock and without creating files. A consumer can touch
$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/agent-warden/notifier to take over desktop notifications while
that heartbeat is fresh. Logs: stdout, which the service sends to the journal.
AGENT_WARDEN_ONLY="pid,pid" restricts every candidate, root or descendant, to
those pids (fixture runs).
"""
